BLOCK|h1|1|Chapter 25: Bootstrapping a Custom Operating System (Cluster OS)
BLOCK|p||For developers and systems architects seeking the ultimate level of native execution, Cluster-lang supports compiling and running applications directly on bare metal without a host operating system layer. This chapter introduces **Cluster OS**, a lightweight, secure, developer-focused operating system configured to run Cluster-lang natively at boot.
BLOCK|p||We will explore how to bootstrap Cluster OS from scratch using a secure Debian 13 (Trixie) base, apply custom systems branding, package it into a compressed SquashFS live-boot file, and compile a bootable hybrid BIOS/EFI ISO.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. Declarative OS Infrastructure vs. Heavy ISO Archives
BLOCK|p||Shipping massive, multi-gigabyte pre-compiled operating system images inside a source code repository introduces extreme storage overhead. To keep the compiler repository size under **100KB**, Cluster-lang introduces a **declarative bootstrapping system**.
BLOCK|p||Instead of storing pre-built binaries, the repository contains a system installer script (`cl-os-builder.py`) and custom tech assets (emblems, wallpapers). When run, the builder fetches system packages dynamically from official Debian repositories and compiles the OS on demand.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. Bootstrapping the System: The 5 Stages
BLOCK|p||The `cl-os-builder.py` script constructs the operating system image inside a temporary workspace directory in five key stages:
BLOCK|h3|3|2.1 Stage 1: Debian 13 RootFS Debootstrap
BLOCK|p||The builder runs the `debootstrap` tool to pull a minimal base filesystem of Debian 13 (Trixie) from official Debian package mirrors:
BLOCK|code|bash|debootstrap --variant=minbase trixie ./rootfs https://deb.debian.org/debian
BLOCK|p||This provisions a secure base of only ~180MB containing the core `apt` package manager, GNU shell utilities, and systemd init hooks.
BLOCK|h3|3|2.2 Stage 2: Installing XFCE4 Desktop & Live Boot Modules
BLOCK|p||The builder enters the guest system namespace (chroot) and installs core desktop packages and live-boot utilities:
BLOCK|code|bash|apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
    linux-image-amd64 systemd-sysv grub-pc live-boot \
    xserver-xorg xinit xfce4 xfce4-terminal \
    lightdm lightdm-gtk-greeter \
    g++ gcc make sqlite3 libsqlite3-dev python3 curl git
BLOCK|list||`live-boot` injects kernel initramfs scripts that search for and mount SquashFS filesystems at boot.
BLOCK|list||`lightdm` provides a clean graphical login panel.
BLOCK|list||`xfce4` provides an ultra-lightweight desktop environment.
BLOCK|h3|3|2.3 Stage 3: Embedding Cluster Compiler Tools
BLOCK|p||The builder copies the active host compiler files directly into `/opt/cluster-lang` inside the guest filesystem and configures symbolic links to expose `cl` and `cl-container` in the system path.
BLOCK|h3|3|2.4 Stage 4: System Branding & Launcher Setup
BLOCK|p||To make the OS look distinct from standard Debian 13:
BLOCK|p||1.  **System files customized:** `/etc/os-release` and `/etc/issue` are modified to register the operating system name as **Cluster OS**.
BLOCK|p||2.  **Greeter & Desktop Wallpapers:** Custom wallpapers and tech emblems are copied to `/usr/share/images/cluster-os/` and mapped to LightDM and XFCE desktop backdrops.
BLOCK|p||3.  **Cluster Terminal Console:** An autostart launcher script opens a customized `xfce4-terminal` running the Cluster Dev Console automatically at user login.
BLOCK|h3|3|2.5 Stage 5: squashfs Compression & ISO Compilation
BLOCK|p||1.  **squashfs Compression:** The builder runs `mksquashfs` on the rootfs directory, compressing the entire system down to a tiny, read-only `/live/filesystem.squashfs` file (~50MB):
BLOCK|code|bash|    mksquashfs ./rootfs ./iso/live/filesystem.squashfs -comp xz -e boot proc sys dev tmp
BLOCK|p||2.  **ISO Packaging:** The kernel and initrd are copied to `/live/`, and `grub-mkrescue` is run to compile the filesystem into a hybrid BIOS/EFI bootable ISO (`cluster-os.iso`).
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|3. Creating a Declarative OS Config (`cluster-os.yaml`)
BLOCK|p||To customize the compiled operating system, write a minimal declarative YAML file in your project root:
BLOCK|code|yaml|# cluster-os.yaml
os_name: Cluster-OS
version: 1.0.0
desktop: xfce                 # xfce, openbox, or headless
base_distro: debian-13        # Debian 13 (Trixie) base

# Additional system packages to install inside the ISO
packages:
  - git
  - curl
  - nano
  - python3

# Command to execute automatically on desktop boot
autostart:
  cmd: "xfce4-terminal -e 'cl-container run nats'"
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|4. How to Build & Live Boot Cluster OS
BLOCK|p||To compile the operating system, you must first install the required building tools on your host:
BLOCK|code|bash|sudo apt install debootstrap xorriso grub-pc-bin grub-efi-amd64-bin mtools squashfs-tools
BLOCK|p||Once installed, execute the bootstrapper:
BLOCK|code|bash|sudo ./cl-os-builder.py
BLOCK|h3|3|4.1 Ventoy Multi-Boot Setup
BLOCK|p||If you use a Ventoy multi-boot USB drive:
BLOCK|p||1.  Simply copy the generated `cluster-os.iso` from your compiler directory.
BLOCK|p||2.  Paste it directly onto your Ventoy USB partition alongside your other ISOs (like Debian or Ubuntu).
BLOCK|p||3.  Reboot your PC, select Ventoy, and choose **Cluster OS** from the menu.
BLOCK|h3|3|4.2 Booting from MicroSD Cards
BLOCK|p||You can boot the operating system using a mobile MicroSD card plugged into a USB adapter:
BLOCK|p||1.  Insert the MicroSD card into your host using the USB adapter.
BLOCK|p||2.  Write the compiled ISO directly to the card:
BLOCK|code|bash|    sudo dd if=cluster-os.iso of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress oflag=sync
BLOCK|p||*(Where `/dev/sdX` is the MicroSD USB adapter device path).*
BLOCK|p||3.  Reboot your PC, press your motherboard boot override key (e.g. F12 or F11), and boot natively into Cluster OS.
BLOCK|hr||
